fact WorkItem(id: "WI-836", description: some(value: "TYPER SOUNDNESS: a parameter whose declared type contains a type VARIABLE accepts ANY argument after the variable's first binding occurrence — two arguments sharing one type param are never checked against each other. MEASURED at HEAD (dcdaf0cd, `anthill load`, 2026-07-28): `operation same[X](a: List[T = X], b: List[T = X]) -> Int64 = 1` called as `same(li(), ls())` with `li() -> List[T = Int64]` and `ls() -> List[T = String]` LOADS CLEAN. Identical result with the 042 bracket seeded explicitly (`same[X = Int64](li(), ls())` — so this is not an inference-only gap), and identical for a SORT-level param: `sort Box { sort T = ?  sort O = ?  entity box(v: T)  operation union(a: Box[T = T, O = O], b: Box[T = T, O = O]) -> Box[T = T, O = O] = a }` with `Box.union(mkq(), mkr())`, `mkq() -> Box[T = Int64, O = Q]`, `mkr() -> Box[T = Int64, O = R]` — clean. CONTROLS, so this is the hole and not the harness: (a) a variable-FREE param mismatch IS caught — `takeR(b: Box[T = Int64, O = R])` applied to a `Box[T = Int64, O = Q]` gives `type mismatch in takeR.b (op-arg): expected Box[T = Int64, O = R], got Box[T = Int64, O = Q]`; (b) the variable DOES bind from argument 1 — `idbox[X](a: List[T = X]) -> List[T = X]` applied to a `List[Int64]` against a declared `List[String]` return errors at go.return with `got List[T = Int64]`; (c) an argument list that constrains X nowhere is loud — `expected a type for 'X', got unconstrained — use pick[X = …](…)`. So binding happens and conformance happens; what is missing is re-checking a LATER argument against the ALREADY-BOUND variable (equivalently: unifying the two arguments' bindings through σ instead of comparing each against the raw declared type). Impact: every generic multi-argument operation silently accepts heterogeneous calls. BLOCKS docs/proposals/058-modular-instances.md §5.3 (WI-648 phase 4): the SortedSet driver's entire safety claim — a `union` of a ByLength set and an Alphabetical one is a TYPE error — rests on this check, and as measured such a union loads clean and merges sets ordered by different comparators. 058 §4.7's evidence was read too broadly: its probe measured the RETURN position, which IS checked. SCOPE: Rust typer, the per-argument conformance path in kb/typing.rs (seed_op_type_args seeds σ; the argument walk must unify against σ and write back, rather than compare against the declared type with the variable left free). ACCEPTANCE: the three probe programs above each fail to load with a diagnostic naming the two conflicting argument bindings and the parameter they disagree on; controls (a)/(b)/(c) still behave exactly as measured; a regression test pins op-level AND sort-level parameters and both the inferred and the explicitly-seeded forms; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-28T10:29:08Z"))

fact Tag(workitem: "WI-836", name: "modinst")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-836", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED. ROOT CAUSE was a WALK DEPTH, one line: validate_arg_against_param (kb/typing.rs, the WI-385 conformance check shared by op-arg / entity-field / function-value sites) resolved both sides with walk_view, which follows only the HEAD var / sort-alias chain and stops at a Term::Fn. A type variable NESTED inside a sort application therefore read as non-ground even once sigma had bound it, and the groundness gate SKIPPED. Fix: retry with the DEEP pure-sigma walk (walk_type_deep_value) on the non-ground verdict. Both spellings closed with one change - op type param (List[T = X]) and the sort's OWN params written out (Box[T = T, O = O]). MEASURED, not predicted: the ticket's third probe (sort-level) is caught only in the WRITTEN spelling; the BARE spelling (union(a: Box, b: Box)) was ALREADY caught by WI-374's enforce_member_tie and still is - the written one reached this hole precisely because a NESTED conflict never attempts a rebind, so it records no contradiction_details for the member tie to read. That asymmetry is now pinned as a control. REMAINING GAP, recorded not papered over: two ARROW-typed arguments sharing one type variable (f: (X) -> Int64, g: (X) -> Int64) are STILL not checked - measured load-clean at delivery and pinned by an inverted known_gap test. The deep walk is deliberately NOT applied when either side is callable: making a callback read as ground routes it from the component-wise arrow checkers (validate_arrow_param_result WI-469 / validate_callback_effect_row WI-440) to the whole-type types_compatible, which REFUSES the Function[A = tuple]-vs-2-parameter-eta-arrow pairing WI-775/WI-792 settled must be ACCEPTED. Measured: without that gate, wi787 (3 cases), wi784's rigid-A case and wi424's row-gated callback all flipped from load-clean to refused, plus wi791/wi792 message drift. Closing the arrow spelling means teaching arrow_compatible_view the Function-states-no-arity rule - a change to who OWNS arrow conformance, not a walk depth, and its own ticket if wanted. WI-844 (058 phase 4 SortedSet driver) is served either way: union(a: SortedSet[T=T,O=O], b: SortedSet[T=T,O=O]) is a sort application, not an arrow.", at: "2026-07-28T10:08:41Z")
